WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.510
The Holderness cliffs have been crumbling
into the North Sea for centuries.

2
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.610
On data going back to 1786, up to 2 million
cubic metres a year has been removed

3
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.890
from the cliffs.

4
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.970
So where does it all go?

5
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.090
Does it pile up at the cliff foot?

6
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.470
Dissolve in the sea?

7
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.190
In this mission, you'll model
the coast as an open system,

8
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.770
with inputs, stores, flows and outputs.

9
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.370
You'll see how feedback can
rebuild a beach after a storm,

10
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.450
or speed up erosion.

11
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.540
Then you'll visit the Dutch coast, where
in 2011 people built a peninsula from

12
00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:39.070
21.5 million cubic metres of sand.

13
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.890
Your mission: find out where the material
eroded from the Holderness cliffs goes.
